# tests/backrefs/octal.rxt -- module `backrefs` ([M6.5.2]): PCRE2's octal disambiguation at the ATOM position: the four ordered questions of S5, cell by cell, each with a DISCRIMINATOR subject so "it compiled" is never mistaken for "it is a backreference"
#
# GENERATED BY tests/backrefs/gen_corpus.py, and that is a property rather
# than a convenience: every expectation below was produced by driving the cell
# through libpcre2 10.46 (via the committed ctypes binding at
# docs/design/backrefs_measurements/probes/br_oracle.py) BEFORE it was written.
# python3 `re` was driven over the SAME cells in the same pass, and a block
# carries `# pcre2-only` exactly where python diverged or could not compile the
# pattern -- DETECTED, never assumed, with the first divergence and the cell
# count recorded above the marking.
#
# THAT MARKING IS THE WHOLE REASON THE GENERATOR EXISTS. This module has the
# largest oracle divergence pcrec has met: python `re` refuses EVERY
# self-reference and EVERY forward reference at compile time, has no `\g`, no
# `\k` and no `(?J)` at all, rejects the `(?<n>...)` declaring spelling, and
# refuses `(?i)` anywhere but the pattern start -- which is exactly where the
# two cells that decide WHERE the caseless flag is read live. R32 C3 found the
# first test plan marking two files python-verifiable in the direction that
# LOSES the oracle; computing the marking removes the species.
#
# Design: docs/design/backrefs_design.md. Divergences are catalogued in
# docs/dev/upstream_issues.md.

# ====================================================================
# RULE 1: `\0` is ALWAYS octal, at most three digits total
# ====================================================================

pattern ^\0$
features backrefs
m "\x00" 0 1
n "0"

pattern ^\012$
features backrefs
m "\n" 0 1
n "\x012"

# Three digits COUNTING THE LEADING 0: `\037` then a literal '7'. A reader who counts three digits AFTER the zero gets 0xff and this cell says so.
pattern ^\0377$
features backrefs
m "\x1f7" 0 2
n "\xff"

# Still octal with a group in scope: there is no group 0 to address, so no ambiguity exists for this row to resolve.
pattern ^(a)\0$
features backrefs
m "a\x00" 0 2
g 1 0 1
n "aa"

# ====================================================================
# RULE 2: one digit 1-9 is ALWAYS a backreference, WHOLE-pattern count
# ====================================================================

# THE ASYMMETRY WITH RULE 3, and no test using only groups-before will see it: `\1` sees the whole pattern, so the group AFTER the escape counts. (The match is nomatch on "aa" because a FORWARD reference is unset when read -- §3.5 -- but the pattern COMPILES, which is the fact this row pins.)
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: "aa" @0 pcre2=('n',) python=('ERR', 'invalid group reference 1 at position 2') (2 of 2 cells)
# pcre2-only
pattern ^\1(a)$
features backrefs
n "aa"
n "\x01a"

pattern ^(a)\1$
features backrefs
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 0 1
n "a\x01"

# `\8` with no group is PCRE2 error 115 -- NOT octal, NOT the literal '8'. The `perr` shape is the assertion.
# pcre2 refuses: pcre2_compile failed at offset 2: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern ^\8$
features backrefs
perr

# pcre2 refuses: pcre2_compile failed at offset 2: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern ^\9$
features backrefs
perr

# `\8` WITH eight groups is a backreference to group 8.
pattern ^(a)(b)(c)(d)(e)(f)(g)(h)\8$
features backrefs
m "abcdefghh" 0 9
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2
g 3 2 3
g 4 3 4
g 5 4 5
g 6 5 6
g 7 6 7
g 8 7 8
n "abcdefgh\x08"

# ====================================================================
# RULE 3: two or more digits, run beginning 1-7 -- SO FAR or OCTAL
# ====================================================================

# ONE group before, so `\10` has no reference reading and is OCTAL 010 = 0x08. Sabotage S-BR8 makes rule 3 count the WHOLE pattern instead, and every groups-before cell still passes -- only this shape and `\10(a)..(j)` below fail.
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: "a\x08" @0 pcre2=('m', 0, 2) python=('ERR', 'invalid group reference 10 at position 5') (2 of 2 cells)
# pcre2-only
pattern ^(a)\10$
features backrefs
m "a\x08" 0 2
g 1 0 1
n "aa0"

# TEN groups AFTER: still OCTAL, because rule 3's count is the one SO FAR. Deferring this decision would let a later group retroactively turn an octal literal into a reference.
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: "\x08abcdefghij" @0 pcre2=('m', 0, 11) python=('ERR', 'invalid group reference 10 at position 2') (2 of 2 cells)
# pcre2-only
pattern ^\10(a)(b)(c)(d)(e)(f)(g)(h)(i)(j)$
features backrefs
m "\x08abcdefghij" 0 11
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
g 3 3 4
g 4 4 5
g 5 5 6
g 6 6 7
g 7 7 8
g 8 8 9
g 9 9 10
g 10 10 11
n "jabcdefghij"

# `\18` is octal `\01` then a LITERAL '8' -- 8 terminates an octal run that has already started, which is a different thing from BEGINNING one (rule 3').
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: "a\x018" @0 pcre2=('m', 0, 3) python=('ERR', 'invalid group reference 18 at position 5') (2 of 2 cells)
# pcre2-only
pattern ^(a)\18$
features backrefs
m "a\x018" 0 3
g 1 0 1
n "aa8"

# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: "\nabcdefghijkl" @0 pcre2=('m', 0, 13) python=('ERR', 'invalid group reference 12 at position 2') (1 of 1 cells)
# pcre2-only
pattern ^\12(a)(b)(c)(d)(e)(f)(g)(h)(i)(j)(k)(l)$
features backrefs
m "\nabcdefghijkl" 0 13

# The octal re-read takes at most three digits and the rest stand for themselves: `\123` then '4'.
pattern ^\1234$
features backrefs
m "S4" 0 2

# ====================================================================
# RULE 3': a run BEGINNING 8 or 9 is ALWAYS a DECIMAL backreference
# ====================================================================

# R32 E3. 8 and 9 are not octal digits, so the octal re-read would consume ZERO digits and produce nothing; PCRE2 reads the whole decimal number instead. With no group 81 this is error 115, and the failure a naive rule 3 produces is a SILENT MIS-PARSE rather than an error. Sabotage S-BR8b.
# pcre2 refuses: pcre2_compile failed at offset 3: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern ^\81$
features backrefs
perr

# pcre2 refuses: pcre2_compile failed at offset 3: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern ^\89$
features backrefs
perr

# pcre2 refuses: pcre2_compile failed at offset 3: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern ^\91$
features backrefs
perr

# ====================================================================
# RULE 4: the octal value ceiling
# ====================================================================

# PCRE2 error 151: the octal value exceeds \377 in 8-bit non-UTF mode. `\4` starts a run with an octal reading, no group 400 exists, and 0400 is out of range.
# pcre2 refuses: pcre2_compile failed at offset 5: octal value is greater than \377 in 8-bit non-UTF-8 mode
pattern ^\400$
features backrefs
perr

pattern ^\377$
features backrefs
m "\xff" 0 1
n "\xfe"

# ====================================================================
# RULE 5: the EXPLICIT forms never take the octal branch
# ====================================================================

# `\g10` with no groups is error 115, not the octal byte 0x08. The `\g` spelling has no octal reading at all.
# pcre2 refuses: pcre2_compile failed at offset 4: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern ^\g10$
features backrefs
perr

# pcre2 refuses: pcre2_compile failed at offset 6: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern ^\g{10}$
features backrefs
perr
